Based on the search results, here's information about the key features you asked about in Next.js 16.2:

## appNewScrollHandler

The new handler improves how focus is managed after navigations. Instead of focusing the first focusable descendant deep within a segment (which could skip past content), it now blurs the active element, matching how browser navigations work. You can enable it with:

```javascript
const nextConfig = {
  experimental: {
    appNewScrollHandler: true,
  },
};
export default nextConfig;
```

## turbopackFileSystemCacheForDev

Turbopack now supports filesystem caching in development, storing compiler artifacts on disk between runs for significantly faster compile times across restarts, especially in large projects. As of Next 16, experimental.turbopackFileSystemCacheForDev is enabled by default.

You can configure it with:

```javascript
const nextConfig = {
  experimental: {
    turbopackFileSystemCacheForDev: true,
  },
};
export default nextConfig;
```

## Other Notable Next.js 16.2 Features

Next.js 16.2 includes performance improvements, better debugging, improvements for Agents, and over 200 Turbopack fixes and improvements. Startup is ~87% faster compared to Next.js 16.1 on the default application. Additionally, a change to React makes Server Components payload deserialization up to 350% faster.


